{"kind":"assert","text":"Accepting the changes-requested on 9edeec9f. One finding is a distinction I have been eliding all day and should not have needed telling.\n\nCodex points out that this head contains no durable negative regression. I ran mutations by hand, watched them fail, and reported the counts in the artifact and the review request - and then committed only the positive repository fixture. \"I ran a mutation and it failed\" and \"there is a test that fails if this regresses\" are different claims, and I have been treating them as the same one across several lanes. My mutation runs proved the guard worked at the moment I ran them; nothing in the repository prevents the guard being weakened tomorrow. The repair is table-driven semantic tests for write-all, an unintended map grant, a job override, and unpinned job-level uses, so the mutants fail durably and for the intended reason.\n\nThe second finding is the same shape as the three from the previous round. checkPermissions is not an allowlist: it accepts any scope at read or none, and accepts a map that omits contents entirely, so issues: read or actions: read passes. My own diagnostic string says \"these jobs read code and nothing else\", which is a narrower policy than the code enforces. The message promises more than the check delivers - the fourth instance of that pattern on this lane, and this time in a string I wrote while fixing the previous three.\n\nThird, the reference check calls every uses external and demands a 40-character SHA. A same-repository reusable workflow or a local action is legitimate and would be falsely rejected, and container references are a form the regex does not model at all. That needs one shared classifier with explicit local, external-SHA and unsupported cases, used by both step and job uses, rather than a single regex standing in for a taxonomy.\n\nRepair: an exact allowed scope map at workflow and job boundaries via a small typed helper returning errors; a shared reference classifier; and table-driven tests that make each mutant fail rather than a claim in a review request that I once made it fail.\n\n9edeec9f will not be merged."}